---
import Card from "./Card/index.astro";
import {LINKS, NEXT_RACE, PATCHES, WELCOME, METADATA} from "../lib/constants";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";
import ThemeSwitcher from "./ThemeSwitcher.astro";
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import portfolioImage from '../assets/me.webp'

/*
 * Mobile hero (below `md`), see the <style> rules at the foot of this file.
 *
 * The portrait bleeds off the card's right edge behind the type. Below `md` it
 * used to be painted at 70% opacity as a flat rectangle, which put mid-grey
 * photo pixels under 20px/300 type: 2.75:1 at worst, i.e. unreadable, and it
 * washed the face out at the same time.
 *
 * The fix separates the two jobs that one opacity value was doing badly at once:
 *
 *   1. `.portrait`'s mask feathers the three edges that fall *inside* the card
 *      (left, top, bottom) so the photo dissolves into the card instead of
 *      cutting a rectangle out of it. The right edge is deliberately not
 *      feathered: it bleeds past the card and the card's clipping handles it.
 *      With the seams gone the photo no longer needs to be knocked back, so it
 *      renders at full opacity and the face is the focal point again.
 *
 *   2. `.intro-type::after` is a scrim: it veils the photo *only* where the
 *      type sits, capping the photo's contribution behind the text at 32%.
 *      It is a pseudo-element of the copy column, not a fixed-size overlay,
 *      so it tracks that column's real width and height — add or lengthen a
 *      WELCOME.description line and the protected area grows with it.
 *
 * Legibility is therefore arithmetic over the theme tokens, not a property of
 * this particular photograph: the worst pixel any image can contain still
 * clears WCAG 1.4.3 in both themes. tests/mobile-hero-contrast.test.ts recomputes
 * that from the built stylesheet.
 *
 * `md:` and up is untouched: the mask and the scrim are both switched off and
 * the portrait goes back in flow with its pink offset shadow.
 *
 * THREE CLASSES HERE EXIST BECAUSE THE CONTROL ROW STOPPED COUNTING COLUMNS, and all
 * three are consequences of one fact: a wrapping row's MAX-content width is every
 * control on a single line (34rem) where the old column grid's was four of them
 * (19rem). The row's minimum shrank, which is the point — but its maximum grew, and
 * this card's copy column is what asks for it. Each was measured before and after.
 *
 *   1. `shrink-0` ON THE PORTRAIT'S WRAPPER. The copy column and the portrait share a
 *      `justify-between` row, so a copy column asking for 34rem takes the difference
 *      out of the photo: the portrait shrank 275 -> 203.48 at `md` and at 1024, i.e.
 *      26%, and by 13-26% at 104 of the 113 widths from 768 to 2560. Pinning the
 *      wrapper makes the copy column absorb the deficit instead, and the portrait
 *      measures 275.00 at every configuration again while the row still wraps to more
 *      controls per line than the ladder allowed.
 *
 *   2. `self-start md:self-auto` ON `.intro-type`. The scrim is a pseudo-element of
 *      the type column, so it is only "over the type" while that column is as wide
 *      as the type. Stretched to the control row's width it dragged across the
 *      portrait — 97.55% of the photo's on-screen width veiled at 360-640 against 81.63% and
 *      0% before — which is the mask's job undone and the photo's focal point with it.
 *      Not stretching below `md` restores the earlier figures exactly.
 *
 *   3. `max-h-full md:max-h-[415px]` ON THE PORTRAIT. Wrapping makes this card
 *      SHORTER, and below `md` the portrait is positioned against the card, so an
 *      absolute cap the card can now undercut leaves the photo overhanging: at 658-767
 *      the card came out 246px against the portrait's 275, and the 15.5px cut off each
 *      end truncated the vertical mask feather, so the photo met the card's inner
 *      edges as a hard rectangle — the exact seam the mask exists to dissolve. Capping
 *      against the card keeps the feather intact; `md:` restores the absolute cap for
 *      the in-flow portrait, which is bounded by its own row instead.
 */
---

<Card colSpan="md:col-span-2 lg:col-span-3" rowSpan="md:row-span-4" fillsArea>
    <div class="flex w-full h-full justify-between md:pr-8">
        <div class="flex flex-col justify-between md:max-h-[18.75rem] gap-4">
            <div class="intro-type flex flex-col h-full self-start md:self-auto">
                {/*
                    ONE TOP-LEVEL HEADING, AND NO EYEBROW. Both halves are the same judgement and both are
                    scored against the frontend-design skill rather than asserted:

                    "welcome" was a level-6 heading — it opened the document's outline at level
                    six, one line above the level-one heading saying "Hi, I'm Calvin". The skill's STRUCTURE IS
                    INFORMATION test asks whether a structural device encodes something true
                    about the content: a heading level is a claim about depth, and level 6
                    above level 1 is a false one. Its writing section asks that each element
                    do exactly ONE job, and the word did none the greeting does not already
                    do — which is the maintainer's own habit of deleting copy the heading
                    implies. Chanel's REMOVE ONE ACCESSORY names it as the accessory: it is
                    the only ornament in the hero and the only string on the page not in
                    constants.ts. Provenance settles the last doubt — `git log -S welcome`
                    traces it to the upstream template author, not to a decision taken here.

                    The two taglines were top-level headings too, so the page shipped THREE. They are not
                    headings of anything: nothing is sectioned under them, and a reader
                    navigating by heading got three top-level entries before the first real
                    one. They keep every class they wore, so THAT half draws nothing new —
                    the change is what the markup CLAIMS, not what it paints.

                    BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT MOVED, because the two halves differ. Measured on
                    real builds either side: at 1440x900 the card stays 824x357 and the
                    document stays 900, so desktop is untouched. On a 390px phone the
                    document goes 1774 -> 1754 — the eyebrow's own line, given back. Deleting
                    a line of copy is not a no-op and should not be described as one.
                */}
                {WELCOME.description.map((item, index) => (index === 0
                    ? <h1 class="m-0 font-light text-xl"><span class={iconClass(WELCOME.greeting_icon)} aria-hidden="true"></span> {item}</h1>
                    : <p class="m-0 font-light text-xl">{item}</p>))}
                {/*
                    THE WHOLE WALL HAD NO DOOR FROM THIS PAGE. `/patches` is in the sitemap and
                    was reachable from nowhere on the home page: the only two internal links are
                    the goal cards' per-sport controls, so seeing every race meant picking a sport
                    you did not ask for and then un-picking it with the "All" chip.

                    IT COSTS NO HEIGHT ON DESKTOP, which is why it goes here rather than into the
                    card grid — but say where it DOES cost, because the two differ. The copy column
                    is `justify-between` against a 275px portrait and had 84px of unspent height at
                    lg; this spends 33 of it and the card measures the same 824x357, document still
                    900. Nothing in the grid budget is touched.

                    On a 390px phone there is no spare column to spend: the card grows 354 -> 387px
                    and the document 1754 -> 1787. Against `main` before this whole change set —
                    which also deleted the eyebrow's line — the net is +13px of mobile document.
                    That is the honest figure; "costs no height" on its own would be a desktop
                    measurement quoted as a page-wide one.

                    Its label is `PATCHES.heading` — the very words the page it opens is headed
                    with — for the same reason EventsLink's label is, and its mark is the mirror
                    of the wall's own way back: `PATCHES.home_icon` is a left arrow on that page,
                    this is the right arrow going out. No new copy and no new icon vocabulary.
                */}
                <p class="m-0 mt-2">
                    <a href="/patches" class="text-link inline-flex items-center gap-2 min-h-6 text-sm">
                        {PATCHES.heading}
                        <span class={`${iconClass(NEXT_RACE.icon)} shrink-0`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </a>
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="control-row">
                <ThemeSwitcher/>
                {LINKS.map(({link, logo, name}) => (<a href={link} target="_blank" class="control">
                    <span class={`${iconClass(logo)} shrink-0`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span class="sr-only">{name}</span>
                </a>))}
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center p-4 shrink-0">
            <Image
                    src={portfolioImage}
                    alt={`${METADATA.name}`}
                    width="275"
                    height="275"
                    densities={[2]}
                    format="webp"
                    class="portrait w-auto max-h-full md:max-h-[415px] select-none absolute right-[-72px] top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 z-[-2] md:relative md:right-auto md:top-auto md:transform-none md:z-auto pointer-events-none md:shadow-[10px_10px_0_var(--shadow)] rounded-[8px]"
                    loading="eager"
            />
        </div>
    </div>
</Card>

<style>
    /* Feathered edges, so the portrait dissolves into the card.
       No -webkit- twins: this project's minifier de-duplicates prefixed
       declarations against their unprefixed originals and drops them, so
       authoring them shipped nothing. An engine without CSS masking simply
       gets no mask — the seams return, but the type stays readable, because
       contrast is the scrim's job and the scrim does not depend on this. */
    .portrait {
        mask-image:
                linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0, #000 100px),
                linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0, #000 56px, #000 calc(100% - 56px), transparent 100%);
        /* Intersect, so an edge is feathered if *either* gradient dims it. */
        mask-composite: intersect;
    }

    .intro-type {
        position: relative; /* containing box for the scrim; NOT a stacking
                               context (z-index stays auto), so the scrim's
                               z-index still resolves against the card. */
    }

    /* The scrim. 68% of the page colour caps the portrait's contribution behind
       the type at 32%, which holds 4.5:1 for *any* photo in either theme — the
       worst case is pure black under #0B0B0B text and pure white under #FAFAFA
       text, i.e. a value derived from the theme tokens rather than tuned to
       today's pixels. It paints one layer above the portrait.

       Both soft edges are cut by the MASK, not by the paint. That matters twice:
       a background-image cannot be transitioned, so a gradient fill snapped to
       the new theme while the card behind it crossfaded — a hard-edged panel
       flashing over the copy on every theme toggle; and forced-colors forces
       background-image off, which would have squared the right edge off there
       too. As a flat background-color it crossfades with the card and the ramp
       survives every mode. */
    .intro-type::after {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        z-index: -1;
        pointer-events: none;
        top: -24px; /* the card's padding: reach its inner edges */
        left: -24px;
        bottom: -56px; /* room for the downward fade to finish below the type */
        width: calc(100% + 76px); /* 24px left bleed + 52px right ramp */
        background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--background) 68%, transparent);
        transition: background-color 0.3s ease; /* match the card's crossfade */
        mask-image:
                linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 calc(100% - 40px), transparent 100%),
                linear-gradient(to right, #000 calc(100% - 44px), transparent 100%);
        mask-composite: intersect;
    }

    /* No color-mix() means the declaration above is dropped whole, which would
       leave the type sitting on an unveiled photo. Fall back to an *opaque*
       band of the card's own colour: the photo stops showing through behind the
       type on those browsers, but the type lands on the same surface as every
       other word on the site. Legibility never depends on color-mix support. */
    @supports not (background-color: color-mix(in srgb, #000 50%, transparent)) {
        .intro-type::after {
            background-color: var(--card-background);
        }
    }

    /* Forced-colors mode: images are exempt from colour forcing, so without an
       opaque surface the type would land straight on the photograph. Repaint it
       so forced CanvasText sits on forced Canvas. The mask still applies here,
       so the right edge stays soft rather than cutting through the portrait. */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        .intro-type::after {
            background-color: Canvas;
        }
    }

    /* Mirrors UnoCSS's `md` breakpoint: desktop keeps the in-flow portrait. That
       breakpoint is text-relative, so this one is too — 48rem is the same 768 CSS
       pixels at the default root size, and it moves with the reader's text where an
       absolute bound would not. If these two ever part company the mobile hero's
       mask and scrim outlive the one-column layout they exist for. */
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
        .portrait {
            mask-image: none;
        }

        .intro-type {
            /* Not merely cosmetic: a positioned element paints in a later phase
               than an in-flow one. Where the md grid squeezes this card the
               last tagline line already overlaps the button row, and leaving
               this positioned flips which of the two paints on top. */
            position: static;
        }

        .intro-type::after {
            display: none;
        }
    }

    /*
     * WITHOUT THIS THE SIX SOCIAL GLYPHS DISAPPEAR IN FORCED COLOURS, and with them the
     * only thing telling the controls apart.
     *
     * Same mechanism EventsLink.astro records for its arrow: presetIcons paints an icon as
     * a MASK over `background-color: currentColor`, and `background-color` is one of the
     * properties a forced-colours mode overrides. Measured on the built page with the mode
     * emulated, against the arrow the repo had already corrected, measured as a control in the same run:
     * that arrow paints rgb(0,0,159) with `forced-color-adjust: none`, while all six of
     * these painted rgb(255,255,255) on a card of rgb(255,255,255) — 1.00:1, erased. These
     * are icon-ONLY controls whose on-screen name IS the glyph, so the row degraded to six
     * identical empty boxes with no way to tell GitHub from LinkedIn from the resume.
     *
     * `[aria-hidden]` IS LOAD-BEARING, for the reason written at length in EventsLink.astro:
     * each anchor also holds an `sr-only` span, so a bare `.control span` would paint the
     * accessible name on its own colour. Scoped by the attribute that makes the mark
     * decorative rather than by a class of its own, which the control allowlist forbids.
     */
    /*
     * AND THE SAME FOR A LINK THAT IS A RUN OF WORDS. `.text-link` carries a mark too — the
     * hero's way to the wall wears the same arrow `EventsLink` wears — and it was erased by
     * the same mechanism while the rule below said "every icon-mask glyph". Measured under forced
     * colours: 784 of 784 pixels of that arrow's box were Canvas, i.e. no ink at all.
     *
     * IT IS A LESSER FAULT THAN THE ONE ABOVE, and worth saying so rather than filing both as
     * one. An icon-only control loses its NAME when its glyph goes; this link keeps the words
     * "My events" and keeps the underline `.text-link` ships, both browser-forced to LinkText
     * on Canvas. What it loses is a signifier it did not need twice. It is repaired because
     * the mark is meant to be there, not because the link stops working without it.
     *
     * LinkText is the right keyword for BOTH: the anchor's own text is forced to LinkText
     * whatever the author says, so painting the mark anything else would make the glyph
     * disagree with the words beside it.
     *
     * SAID AGAIN IN 404.astro AND patches/[...sport].astro, because Astro scopes a component's
     * styles to that component and all three pages own a `.text-link` carrying a mark. That
     * duplication is the same one `a.control` already lives with.
     */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        a.control span[aria-hidden],
        .text-link span[aria-hidden] {
            forced-color-adjust: none;
            background-color: LinkText;
        }
    }
</style>
